\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE CHECK\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBase rates applied (reference rates from the framework, kept as priors):\n- 5-year survival for a scaled, cash-generative incumbent: ~95%\n- Sustaining >10% revenue growth at $3B revenue base for 5 years: ~25\u201335%\n- Sustaining >6% growth at this scale: ~50\u201365%\n- Moat-protected niche leader maintaining share >60% over 5 years: ~60\u201370%\n\nDeviation from base rate: My scenario set implies a 75% probability of \u22656% revenue\ngrowth through 2030 (base + bull) and a 25% probability of >10% sustained growth.\nThe \u22656% probability sits at the upper end of the base-rate range. Two\ncompany-specific justifications: (1) regulatory lock-in: switching a primary\npackaging component after regulatory approval requires a supplemental filing \u2014\nthis is a durable structural barrier; (2) GLP-1/category volumes are a genuine\ndemand inflection (high-teens HVP growth in Q2-2026, double-digit expected\n[Estimate \u2014 earnings call transcript via Investing.com]).\n\nBase-rate shock check: my bull case (12% revenue CAGR) is ~25% probability vs a\n~15\u201320% base rate for sustained 12% growth at this scale \u2014 within 1.5x, not a red\nflag, but it is a monitoring item. No scenario exceeds 2.0x the base rate.\n\nBayesian update (Layer 4.3, prior\u2192posterior): Prior P(long-term \u22658% growth) \u2248 30%.\nEvidence: two FY2026 guidance raises (company), GLP-1 high-teens growth, HVP mix\nimprovement. Likelihood ratio \u2248 1.8 \u2192 Posterior \u2248 43%. This is consistent with my\nbull + half-of-base weights (~50%) and does not warrant a re-set.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2-1 Business understanding (3 sentences): WST sells the rubber/elastomer\nstoppers, plungers, seals and self-injection devices that pharma companies must\nuse to package injectable drugs. Because packaging components are part of the\nregulatory filing, once a drug is approved with West components, switching is\nexpensive and slow. Recurring demand is therefore anchored to the global installed\nbase of injectable biologics, vaccines and increasingly GLP-1 therapies.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat Score (7 types, 0\u20133 each):\n- Intangible assets (brand trust, regulatory dossiers): 3\n- Switching costs (regulatory re-filing to change components): 3\n- Network effects: 0\n- Cost advantage: 2\n- Economies of scale: 2\n- Regulatory barriers: 3\n- Data moat (validation/regulatory data): 2\nTOTAL: 15/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT\nMoat direction: STABLE, with narrowing risks from Gerresheimer/SGD Pharma capacity\ninvestment [Estimate \u2014 research brief] and tariff/reshoring pressure.\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch classification: FAST GROWER (borderline Stalwart): 2026 organic\ngrowth of 10\u201311% [Actual \u2014 company guidance], but with cyclicality in pharma\ncapital spending (revenue actually declined \u20131.9% in FY2024). Core checkpoint:\nsustainability of the growth rate beyond 2026 \u2014 this is the pivotal variable.\n\nStep 2-4 Financial strength scorecard (all $B, [Actual/Arithmetic] unless noted):\n- Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u2013FY25): +2.1% | Last 4Q vs prior 4Q: +15.0%\n- Operating margin: 3-yr avg 19.2%; latest TTM 19.0%\n- EPS: FY2021 $8.67 [Arithmetic] \u2192 FY2025 $6.80 [Arithmetic] (declining; recovering in 2026)\n- ROE (FY25): 15.6% | ROIC (FY25, GAAP): \u224819%\n- FCF margin FY25: 15.3%; FCF conversion (FCF/NI): 95%\n- Net debt/EBITDA: NET CASH (\u2013$0.47B using vendor debt); interest coverage \u224839x\n- Balance sheet: net cash, low leverage\nVERDICT: EXCELLENT\n\nStep 2-5 Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each):\n- Buyback efficiency: 2 (consistent buyback: shares 76.3M FY21 \u2192 72.7M FY25;\n  net issuance \u20131.4% 1-yr, but no evidence of trough buying)\n- M&A track record: 2 (no large transformative deals; small tuck-ins)\n- Capex efficiency: 2 (capex elevated through 2023\u201324, now moderating; incremental\n  ROIC likely above WACC but not spectacular)\n- Rationality of mix: 2\n- Guidance accuracy: 2 (raised 2026 guidance twice \u2014 positive signal)\nInsider bonuses: CEO insider ownership 0.64% (no bonus); recent 6-mo insider\nactivity [Unconfirmed].\nTOTAL: 10/15 \u2014 acceptable, not exemplary.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2.5-1 R&D capitalization: R&D is not separately broken out in the pack\n(SG&A includes it). Assumed 2.8% of revenue \u2248 $0.086B FY25 [Assumption].\nCapitalizing over 4 years: unamortised R&D \u2248 $0.22B [Assumption].\nStep 2.5-2 SBC treatment: FY25 SBC $0.024B [Filed]. Adjusted FCF = 0.469 \u2013 0.024\n= $0.445B [Arithmetic]. SBC is ~5% of FCF, not material.\nStep 2.5-3 Operating leases: $0.118B [Filed]. Adjusted net debt \u2248 cash 0.791 \u2013\n(LT debt 0.203 + finance leases 0.005 + op leases 0.118) = +$0.465B net cash\n[Arithmetic/Estimate]. This is consistent with the vendor's total debt of $0.321B\n\u2014 treating the vendor figure as the total including leases [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC \u2248 NOPAT (~0.49B) / (IC 2.59B + unamortised R&D 0.22B)\n\u2248 17.5\u201318.5% vs GAAP ~19% \u2192 gap <5pct; no material distortion.\nStep 2.5-6 Owner earnings (FY25):\n- Income-statement route: NI 0.494 + D&A 0.171 \u2013 maintenance capex 0.150 [Assumption]\n  \u2013 \u0394WC 0.336 = $0.179B\n- Cash-flow route: OCF 0.755 \u2013 SBC 0.024 \u2013 maintenance capex 0.150 = $0.581B\nThe wide gap reflects the 2025 working-capital build, which I treat as partly a\ndata-discontinuity artefact (see Section 10) and partly a genuine inventory build.\nFor DCF I use a normalised \u0394WC of zero \u2014 explicitly conservative and flagged.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n3.1 DISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION\n- Risk-free: 4.71% [Actual \u2014 Trading Economics/CNBC 2026-08-21]\n- Equity risk premium: 4.5% [Assumption]\n- Beta: 1.00 [Assumption \u2014 pack beta 1.138 is window-unstated; healthcare\n  defensive franchise supports \u22481.0; using 1.0 avoids over-stating the discount rate]\n- Cost of equity: 4.71 + 1.00 \u00d7 4.5 = 9.2%\n- Cost of debt: pre-tax 5.0% [Assumption] \u00d7 (1 \u2013 22%) = 3.9%\n- Capital weights: E = 24.857/(24.857+0.321) = 98.7%; D = 1.3%\n- WACC = 98.7% \u00d7 9.2% + 1.3% \u00d7 3.9% = 9.13% \u2192 ROUNDED TO 9.0%\nSensitivity to WACC shown below.\n\n3.2 EXPLICIT FORECAST (BASE CASE, $B) \u2014 SOURCE MAP BELOW THE TABLE\n| FY        | Revenue | Growth | OI marg | EBIT  | NOPAT\u00b9 | D&A   | Capex | \u0394WC | FCF   |\n|-----------|---------|--------|---------|-------|--------|-------|-------|-----|-------|\n| 2026E     | 3.362   | +9.4%  | 21.5%   | 0.723 | 0.607  | 0.170 | 0.219 | 0.0 | 0.559 |\n| 2027E     | 3.631   | +8.0%  | 21.8%   | 0.792 | 0.665  | 0.185 | 0.236 | 0.0 | 0.614 |\n| 2028E     | 3.885   | +7.0%  | 22.0%   | 0.855 | 0.718  | 0.198 | 0.252 | 0.0 | 0.664 |\n| 2029E     | 4.118   | +6.0%  | 22.2%   | 0.914 | 0.768  | 0.212 | 0.268 | 0.0 | 0.712 |\n| 2030E     | 4.324   | +5.0%  | 22.4%   | 0.969 | 0.814  | 0.228 | 0.281 | 0.0 | 0.760 |\n\u00b9 NOPAT = EBIT \u00d7 (1 \u2013 0.16). Tax rate 16% [Assumption, derived from the pack's\ninternal consistency: OI/NI relationship implies a low-teens-to-high-teens rate;\n16% is a conservative central pick].\n\nTerminal (2031+): g = 4.0% [Assumption \u2014 in line with long-term nominal GDP];\nterminal ROIC = 25% [Assumption, justified by wide moat / 70% share] \u2192\nreinvestment rate = g/ROIC = 16%.\nTerminal FCF = 0.814 \u00d7 1.04 \u00d7 (1 \u2013 0.16) = 0.711\nTerminal value = 0.711 / (0.09 \u2013 0.04) = $14.22B\n\nPV of explicit FCF = 0.559/1.09 + 0.614/1.09\u00b2 + 0.664/1.09\u00b3 + 0.712/1.09\u2074 + 0.760/1.09\u2075\n                  = 0.513 + 0.517 + 0.512 + 0.504 + 0.494 = $2.54B\nPV of terminal value = 14.22 / 1.09\u2075 = 14.22 \u00d7 0.650 = $9.24B\nEnterprise value = $11.78B\nNet cash = 0.791 \u2013 0.321 = +$0.47B [Arithmetic using vendor total debt]\nEquity value = $12.25B\nBASE-CASE INTRINSIC VALUE = $12.25B / 70.4M = $174.0 [Estimate]\n\nFORECAST SOURCE MAP (as requested):\n- Revenue growth: FY2026 = guidance midpoint $3.362B, +10\u201311% organic\n  [Actual \u2014 PR Newswire via investor.westpharma.com, Q2-2026 release].\n  FY2027 = +8%, FY2028 +7%, FY2029 +6%, FY2030 +5% \u2014 ALL ARE MY ASSUMPTIONS.\n  NO company guidance exists beyond 2026 [Unconfirmed \u2014 searched; only sell-side\n  consensus (FY27 EPS $9.58, MarketWatch) was found]. The deceleration path is\n  designed to converge to base-rate norms for a $3B+ compounder; it is\n  deliberately NOT a naive extension of the 2026 guide.\n- Operating margin: Starting at 21.5% (blend of GAAP TTM 19.0% [Filed] and\n  vendor-adjusted 22.5% [Aggregator]); rising to 22.4% by 2030 [Assumption \u2014\n  assumes HVP mix recovery but NOT a return to 2022's 25.4% peak].\n- Capex: 6.5% of revenue, down from FY25 9.3% [Filed]. Q2-2026 capex was $43M\n  vs $75M a year earlier [Actual \u2014 Investing.com transcript], supporting\n  moderation. 6.5% is my assumption.\n- D&A: ~5% of revenue, consistent with FY25 $0.171B [Filed]. Assumption.\n- \u0394WC: 0 (data discontinuity; see audit). Assumption, conservative.\n\n3.3 TORNADO SENSITIVITY (\u00b11\u03c3): TOP 3 VARIABLES \u2192 THESIS KPIs\n1. 2027\u20132030 revenue CAGR (4% to 10%): IV \u2248 $135 \u2192 $250\n   \u2192 KPI: HVP quarterly organic growth; GLP-1 revenue share.\n2. WACC (8.0% to 10.0%): IV \u2248 $215 \u2192 $137\n   \u2192 KPI: 10Y yield + Fed path (macro monitor).\n3. FY2030 operating margin (20.5% to 25.0%): IV \u2248 $145 \u2192 ~$215\n   \u2192 KPI: gross margin and price/mix; tariff/reshoring cost headwind.\n\n3.4 MULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK\n- Trailing P/E: 45.0x [Aggregator]; Forward P/E (2026E GAAP EPS ~8.44): 41.8x\n- EV/EBITDA (FY25): EV 24.39 / (EBITDA 0.756) = 32.3x \u2014 extremely rich\n- P/FCF (FY25): 24.86 / 0.469 = 53.0x\n- P/B: 8.29x [Aggregator] | PEG: 3.04 [Aggregator]\n- 10-yr average P/E: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not in pack]\nMultiple-based conclusions are not used in isolation (framework rule). But at\n41.8x forward earnings with a consensus EPS growth rate of ~11%, PEG >3 \u2014 the\nmultiple alone does not support a buy.\n\n3.5 REVERSE DCF (what the market price implies)\n$353.08 \u2192 market EV $24.39B. With WACC 9%, terminal g 4%, terminal P/E 30x on\n2030 GAAP EPS, the price is consistent with:\n- GAAP EPS ~$8.44 (2026) growing ~17%/yr to ~$15.9 by 2030, AND a 30x exit \u2014 OR \u2014\n- a 10\u201311% revenue CAGR through 2030 and a 35x+ exit multiple.\nBoth paths sit at or above the top of my feasible range (bull case). This is the\ncore of the valuation verdict.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3.5-1 Competitive response: If WST's GLP-1 packaging success continues,\nGerresheimer, SGD Pharma, BD and Datwyler will add elastomer/prefillable\ncapacity; price pressure in commodity components is likely within 3 years\n[Estimate \u2014 research brief]. Impact: \u20132.\n3.5-2 Reflexivity: Positive loop exists but is secondary \u2014 a high multiple makes\nSBC cheap and supports customer confidence; the 2026 cyber incident cuts the\nother way (customer trust risk). Impact: +1.\n3.5-3 Customer behaviour: GLP-1s are moving from early adopter to mainstream \u2014\nthis supports volume but brings pricing scrutiny as generics launch (\"benefits\nfrom generic GLP-1 launches\" \u2014 management commentary [Actual \u2014 transcript quote]).\nImpact on LTV/CAC: neutral-to-negative for margins over time. Impact: 0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| Scenario | Prob | Revenue CAGR 2026\u201330 | Terminal OM | WACC | IV/share |\n|----------|------|----------------------|-------------|------|----------|\n| BEAR     | 25%  | +2.5%                | 20.5%       | 9.5% | $126     |\n| BASE     | 50%  | +6.5%                | 22.4%       | 9.0% | $174     |\n| BULL     | 25%  | +12.0%               | 23.5%       | 8.5% | $275     |\nProbabilities sum to 100. Base is the single highest-probability scenario.\nExpected value = 0.25\u00d7126 + 0.50\u00d7174 + 0.25\u00d7275 = $187 [Estimate].\n50% CI: $155\u2013$215 | 80% CI: $120\u2013$270.\nMargin of Safety (base IV $174): (174 \u2013 353)/174 = \u2013103% \u2192 NONE (negative).\nMargin of Safety (expected IV $187): \u201389% \u2192 NONE.\nBayesian posterior after 2026 evidence = 43% probability of \u22658% long-term growth\n(consistent with scenario weights; no further adjustment).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMarket-implied horizon: current price embeds ~5 years of high-teens EPS growth\nand a 30x+ terminal multiple (reverse DCF).\nMy horizon: 3 years (holding-period default).\nSpread: negative \u2014 the market's horizon is longer AND more optimistic than mine.\nNO POSITIVE SPREAD \u2192 no horizon arbitrage opportunity. This is a genuine\ninvestment finding, not an artefact of model choice.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConviction score (out of 15):\n- Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, excellent balance sheet)\n- Valuation attractiveness: 0/3 (negative margin of safety, price > bull IV)\n- Macro alignment: 1/3 (reflationary regime is mild headwind for long-duration)\n- Thesis clarity: 2/3 (KPIs measurable: HVP growth, margins, WestVantage trough)\n- Execution traceability: 2/3 (strong multi-quarter record, but Q2-2026 cyber\n  incident + CEO retirement add uncertainty)\nTOTAL: 8/15 \u2192 CONVICTION: MEDIUM \u2192 max position 5\u20136% at fair entry.\nConviction decay: \u20131 per 6 months \u2192 effective ~7/15 for a 1-year holding period.\nSizing: at