ess drag |\n| Liquidity | 0.0 | Healthcare supplier demand is liquidity-insensitive |\n| Economic cycle | \u22120.5 | Elective procedure/drug-volume sensitivity; GLP-1 secular demand offsets |\n| Commodity | 0.0 | Modest petrochemical/glass input exposure, pricing power offsets |\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment\nExpected macro impact = weighted average across regimes \u2248 \u22120.7 / neutral-to-slight-headwind. Macro does not change IV; it lowers entry urgency and reinforces a limit-order approach. Macro alignment: HEADWIND \u2192 Conviction \u22120.5 steps.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates\n- 5-year survival probability for a regulatory-lock-in component supplier with ~70% niche share: >90% [Base rate, qualitative]\n- Probability a company with WST's archetype compounds revenue >10% for 10 years: ~15\u201325% [Base rate \u2014 large-cap manufacturing niches typically fade to mid-single-digit]\n- Platform-transition success rate (installed-base monetization): ~20\u201330% [Base rate] \u2014 WST is already 'done' with the transition, so this base rate does not bite as a forward risk\n- Cyclical-peak 40% drawdown within 3 years: ~30\u201340% \u2014 the 2022\u201325 drawdown ($350 to $223, \u221236%) already occurred [Base rate]\n- Large pharma capex cycles mean-revert; packaging capacity added in booms creates price pressure in busts [Base rate]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation vs base rate\nThe bull case requires revenue CAGR >10% for a decade. Company-specific justifications to deviate upward from the 15\u201325% base rate:\n1. 70% share in elastomeric closures with drug-specific regulatory validation = structural switching cost that preserves pricing [Actual \u2014 research brief]\n2. GLP-1 drug demand is a genuine multi-year volume supercycle (capacity is the sector bottleneck) [Estimate \u2014 PharmaNow]\n3. HVP mix improving, and 2026 guidance has been raised twice (5\u20137% \u2192 10\u201311% organic) [Actual \u2014 investor presentations/transcripts]\nJustification sufficient to move the bull probability to 30%, but not to the 45%+ that a 44\u00d7 P/E requires.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check\n- Bull probability (30%) is 1.2\u20132.0\u00d7 the base rate \u2192 requires additional evidence. The evidence exists but is time-limited: three quarters of raised guidance.\n- The market-implied growth (see Layer 3, reverse DCF) is 12\u201315% revenue CAGR for a decade \u2014 that is ~2.0\u00d7+ the base rate \u2192 RED FLAG. Mandatory re-evaluation content: the current price itself fails this check.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences)\nWST designs and manufactures drug containment and delivery systems \u2014 elastomeric stoppers and seals, polymer and glass vials, prefillable syringes, and self-injection platforms \u2014 sold to pharma and biotech manufacturers. The moat is the regulatory validation lock-in: once a drug is approved and commercialized with West components, switching suppliers is expensive and slow, creating high-retention annuity revenue. Growth is now powered by high-value products (HVP), particularly packaging for GLP-1 drugs and injectable biologics.\nUnderstandability: HIGH\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment\n| Moat type | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (regulatory files, brand trust) | 2 |\n| Switching costs (drug-specific validation) | 3 |\n| Network effects | 0 |\n| Cost advantage | 2 |\n| Economies of scale | 2 |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 |\n| Data moat | 1 |\n| **Total** | **13/21 = WIDE MOAT** |\n\nMoat direction: STABLE, slightly WIDENING on HVP/GLP-1 content (complex elastomeric components raise switching costs), but NARROWING RISK from emerging competitors (BD, Gerresheimer, SGD Pharma adding ready-to-use capacity) [Estimate]. Net: Stable.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nFast Grower with residual cyclicality (revenue re-accelerated from a post-COVID trough: +4 quarters of 7.5\u201321% YoY growth). Checkpoints: (1) inventory growth must track sales \u2014 inventory rose 18% in 2025, watch; (2) earnings momentum: TTM net income +14% vs 2025; (3) niche dominance: 70% elastomeric share \u2014 passing.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard [Actual \u2014 filings unless noted]\n- Revenue growth: 3-yr CAGR (2022\u201325) +2.1%; 5-yr CAGR +7.4%; TTM revenue $3.327B (+8.2% vs FY2025)\n- Operating margin: FY2025 19.0%; TTM 19.0%; 12-yr median 17.5%\n- Net income: FY2025 $494M; TTM $565M\n- ROE: 19.1% (vendor, FY2025 basis); ROIC ~18% [Estimate \u2014 see Section 2.5]\n- FCF margin: FY2025 15.3%; TTM 13.1%\n- Net debt: net cash \u2248 $470M [Estimate \u2014 see Section 3 debt decision]\n- Interest coverage: >30\u00d7 (EBIT $585M vs interest $15M)\n- Scorecard verdict: EXCELLENT\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard\n1. Buyback efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 net buyback \u22121.4% shares in 2025, but modest; no large-scale below-multiple buying\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 no major integrated M&A to handicap; organic capex-led growth\n3. Capex efficiency: 1/3 \u2014 FY2023\u201325 capex $1.025B produced only +$124M revenue; incremental ROIC negative over that window [Arithmetic \u2014 filings]. Long cycle, but the 2024\u201325 trough explains it\n4. Mix rationality: 3/3 \u2014 no empire building; dividends + buybacks + organic capacity in the growth line\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 consecutive beats in Q1'26 (+25\u201335% EPS per Van Hessen) and Q2'26 (+13.9% per Zacks), raised guidance twice in 2026\nInsider adjustment: 0 pts (CEO/mgmt ownership 0.64% \u2014 low; no 6-mo insider cluster buys or sells reported [Unconfirmed])\n**Total: 10/15** \u2014 GOOD\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- R&D capitalization [Assumption]: R&D is not carved out in this pack; impute ~$100M/yr (\u22483% of revenue, in SG&A). Capitalized over 4 years, steady-state unamortized R&D asset \u2248 $200M. Adjusted OI = 585 + 100 \u2212 50 = $635M. Adjusted NOPAT \u2248 635 \u00d7 0.84 = $533M.\n- SBC adjustment: FY2025 SBC $24M; it is already expensed in GAAP OI, so no further adjustment is needed to GAAP-based NOPAT. TTM FCF $437M is approximately SBC-adjusted already (SBC in OCF add-back).\n- Operating leases: $118M present value (FY2025). Debt + leases total \u2248 $321M (reconciles with vendor total debt). Net cash \u2248 791 \u2212 321 = $470M.\n- Adjusted ROIC: Adjusted IC = 3,176 + 321 \u2212 791 + 200 \u2248 $2.906B \u2192 Adjusted ROIC \u2248 533/2,906 \u2248 18.3%. GAAP ROIC \u2248 491/2,706 \u2248 18.1%. Gap < 5pp \u2192 no distortion.\n- Owner Earnings (FY2025) = NI $494M + D&A $171M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$71M [Assumption, ~25% of capex] \u2212 \u0394operating WC $29M [Arithmetic: WC ex-cash 2024 $503M \u2192 2025 $532M] = **$565M \u2248 $8.03/share** \u2192 Price/Owner Earnings = 44\u00d7.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 BLENDED ENGINE (Engine 3 \u00d7 60% + Engine 1 \u00d7 40%)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u25