| 1.170 | 0.983 | 0.286 | 0.354 | 0.048 | 0.867 |\n| 2033 | 5.51 | 22.5% | 1.240 | 1.042 | 0.303 | 0.375 | 0.047 | 0.923 |\n| 2034 | 5.81 | 22.5% | 1.307 | 1.098 | 0.320 | 0.395 | 0.045 | 0.978 |\n| 2035 | 6.10 | 22.5% | 1.373 | 1.153 | 0.336 | 0.415 | 0.044 | 1.030 |\n\n**DCF computation (mid-year convention, WACC 9.75%)**\n- PV of FCFF 2026\u201335 \u2248 $4.66B [Arithmetic]\n- Terminal value = 1.030 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.0975 \u2212 0.03) = $15.16B \u2192 PV = $6.26B\n- EV = $10.92B; equity = EV + net cash $0.47B = $11.39B\n- **Base-case intrinsic value = $162/share** [Estimate]\n- 50% CI: $145\u2013$190 [Assumption, reflecting WACC 9.25\u201310.25% and growth \u00b11pp]\n- 80% CI: $120\u2013$225 [Assumption, including a GLP-1 upside case and a competitive-margin fade]\n\n**Engine 3 platform-premium overlay** \u2014 the regulatory lock-in justifies a higher multiple than a pure component supplier. Apply a 0.4\u00d7(EV/Sales) + 0.4\u00d7(EV/GP) + 0.2\u00d7(peer) composite with a platform premium of +10\u201315% [Assumption]. This lifts the blended value to a **Base IV of $230 [Estimate]** (blend: 60% \u00d7 230 + 40% \u00d7 162 = $203). I present the disciplined blended midpoint as **Base IV \u2248 $200** [Assumption], recognizing that reasonable-people variation spans $160\u2013$240.\n\n**Reverse DCF (market-implied expectations)**\n- Current price $353 on 2026 net sales $3.322B = 7.5\u00d7 EV/Sales; 39\u201340\u00d7 2026 adjusted EPS; 45\u00d7 trailing GAAP EPS.\n- Reverse DCF: the price requires **12\u201315% revenue CAGR for 10 years**, 24%+ EBIT margins, and a 9\u20139.75% WACC, with no margin-of-safety. Verdict: EXCESSIVE to UNREALISTIC.\n- Multiple cross-check: at a mature quality-compounder multiple of 28\u201330\u00d7 on 2029 EPS of ~$10.7 [Estimate], the 2029 fair value is $300\u2013$321 \u2014 i.e., buying at $353 today implies ~0% compound return over three years even in the decent case.\n\n**Tornado (Top-3 drivers, \u00b11\u03c3)**\n1. Revenue CAGR (7.2% \u00b1 1.5pp) \u2192 IV $130\u2013$260\n2. Terminal EBIT margin (22.5% \u00b1 1.5pp) \u2192 IV $170\u2013$235\n3. WACC (9.75% \u00b1 0.5pp) \u2192 IV $185\u2013$225\nTop-3 thesis KPIs: HVP organic growth; gross margin; capex/revenue.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- Competitive response (\u22122): If WST's GLP-1 success becomes visible enough to justify its own premium, BD/Gerresheimer/SGD will add elastomeric + RTU capacity on accelerated timelines; price competition in closures historically lags capacity by 18\u201324 months [Estimate].\n- Reflexivity (+1): High equity value funds GLP-1 capacity with cheap capital and attracts talent; a de-rating is slower to bite because WST is net-cash and OCF-covered.\n- Customer evolution (neutral-to-positive): Early adopters (GLP-1 innovators) \u2192 mainstream (generic GLP-1 entrants by 2027\u201329) expands the addressable installer base; LTV/CAC improves because incremental customers require no new R&D, but per-unit pricing in generics is lower. Net: volume up, mix down\u2014neutral for margins [Assumption].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u255