ITDA \u2248 0.6x [Estimate].\n\n2.5-4. Brand/data assets: not material; regulatory qualification serves the brand function. [Assumption]\n\n2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: NOPAT FY2025 = $0.585B \u00d7 (1\u22120.22) = $0.456B [Arithmetic]; adjusted invested capital = equity $3.176B + debt/leases $0.708B \u2212 cash $0.791B = $3.093B [Arithmetic]. Adjusted ROIC \u2248 17\u201319% (with lease add-back ~19\u201321%) vs GAAP ROIC \u2248 15.6%. Gap ~4pp, explained by lease capitalization and cash drag. [Estimate]\n\n2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett): NI $494M + D&A $171M \u2212 maintenance capex (~$200M [Assumption]) \u2212 \u0394WC_CFS (~$66M [Estimate]) = ~$399M. [Estimate]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n3-1. Discount-rate derivation (9.1%)\n- Risk-free: 4.70% [Actual \u2013 FRED 2026-08-24]\n- ERP: 4.50% [Assumption \u2013 long-term U.S. estimate]\n- Beta: 1.00 [Assumption \u2013 pack beta 1.138 [Estimate] on unstated window; we moderate to 1.00; see sensitivity]\n- K_e = 4.70 + 1.00 \u00d7 4.50 = 9.20% [Arithmetic]\n- K_d pre-tax: 5.20% [Assumption; investment-grade rated med-tech, BBB\u2013A]\n- Tax rate: 22% [Assumption \u2013 pack-withheld; identity holds in all tested years]\n- Debt weight: 0.8% (market value; $0.208B debt vs $24.857B equity)\n- WACC = 0.992 \u00d7 9.20% + 0.008 \u00d7 5.20% \u00d7 0.78 = 9.1% [Arithmetic]\n\n3-2. Explicit forecast (base case)\nSources: FY2026 guidance [Actual]; FY2027 consensus growth +6.3% revenue, +11.2% EPS [Estimate \u2013 pack Section 8, stale 15 days]; post-2027 taper [Assumption]; capex $250\u2013275M FY26 [Actual \u2013 guidance]; post-2026 capex/D&A [Assumption]; WC net drain [Assumption].\nAll $ millions unless noted.\n\n| FY | Revenue | Growth | EBIT margin | EBIT | NOPAT | +D&A | +Other* | =OCF | Capex | FCFF |\n|----|---------|--------|-------------|------|-------|------|---------|------|-------|------|\n| 2026 | 3,360 | +9.3% | 19.5% | 655 | 511 | 185 | 67 | 763 | 262 | 501 |\n| 2027 | 3,572 | +6.3% | 20.0% | 714 | 557 | 196 | 71 | 824 | 293 | 531 |\n| 2028 | 3,768 | +5.5% | 20.5% | 773 | 603 | 203 | 75 | 881 | 309 | 572 |\n| 2029 | 3,956 | +5.0% | 21.0% | 831 | 648 | 210 | 79 | 937 | 320 | 617 |\n| 2030 | 4,134 | +4.5% | 21.0% | 868 | 677 | 215 | 83 | 975 | 331 | 644 |\n\n*Other = net of SBC, deferred taxes, and working-capital investment; \u22482% of revenue [Assumption].\nCapex: FY2026 $262M [Actual guidance midpoint]; FY27\u2013FY30 at 8.2%\u20138.0% of revenue [Assumption]. D&A: 5.5% \u2192 5.2% of revenue [Assumption].\nTerminal: g = 3.5% (long-run nominal GDP + healthcare mix) [Assumption]; terminal FCFF growth = 3.5%.\n\n3-3. DCF result\nPV of explicit FCFF (WACC 9.1%):\n2026: $460M | 2027: $446M | 2028: $441M | 2029: $436M | 2030: $417M \u2192 PV-sum = $2.200B [Arithmetic]\n\nTerminal value (end-2030): TV = 644 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.091 \u2212 0.035) = $11.911B | PV(TV) = $7.711B [Arithmetic]\nEV = $9.911B | + Net cash $0.583B | = Equity $10.494B | \u00f7 70.4M = **$149/share** [Arithmetic]\n\n3-4. Multiples cross-check\n- FY2026E adjusted EPS $8.95 [Actual guidance midpoint].\n- At 25x forward EPS (historically normal for quality med-tech, [Assumption]) \u2192 $224.\n- At 30x (premium to history, [Assumption]) \u2192 $269.\n- EV/EBITDA FY2026E = 27\u201328x currently; 20x (still premium) implies EV $16.8B \u2192 ~$247. [Arithmetic]\n\n3-5. Reverse DCF: At $353.08, the market implies FCFF compounding ~20%+ for a decade, or an exit multiple >35x on a long-run EPS path of $10\u201312, or WACC below 6%. Each of these is outside our credible range.\n\n3-6. Blended intrinsic value: 60% DCF ($149) + 40% multiples ($224\u2013$269) = **$180\u2013$200** [Arithmetic]. With scenario probability weighting from Layer 4, we set point IV = **$220**, 50% CI $170\u2013$280, 80% CI $140\u2013$380.\n\n3-7. Tornado sensitivity (\u00b11\u03c3 on key variables; impact on IV)\n- WACC 9.1% \u00b1 1.0pp \u2192 IV \u00b1~$35\n- FCF margin \u00b1 1pp \u2192 IV \u00b1~$20\n- Terminal growth \u00b1 0.5pp \u2192 IV \u00b1~$14\n- FY27 growth \u00b1 2pp \u2192 IV \u00b1~$12\nTop-3 thesis KPIs: 1) WACC/rates; 2) HVP margin mix; 3) FCF conversion (working-capital intensity).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n3.5-1. Competitive response: Datwyler, Gerresheimer, and SGD Pharma are adding elastomer/ready-to-use capacity [Estimate \u2013 web research]; price competition in standard products is rising, but high-value components are qualification-gated. Counter-move intensity: \u22121.\n\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: High share price + big buyback + SBC \u2192 talent retention and customer reliability perception reinforce each other. A drawdown in the stock would invert this (customer concern about continuity, SBC dilution). Current status: positive reflexivity. [Assumption]\n\n3.5-3. Customer behaviour evolution: Early-adopter GLP-1 volume is shifting to mainstream; WST benefits because injectable formats are expanding (peptide volumes, auto-injectors). Risk: dose-spacing, oral GLP-1 entrants, or device competition could slow device attach rates after 2028. [Assumption]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n4-1/4-2. Scenario set (probabilities sum 100%)\n\nScenario A \u2014 Bull: \u201cGLP-1 Golden Decade\u201d (25%)\n- HVP sustains 15%+ organic; margins hit 25% by 2029; EPS FY2029 $13.5\u2013$14.5.\n- Exit 32x forward EPS \u2192 $450\u2013$550 by mid-2029; discounted at 9% \u2192 **$385\u2013$430** now.\n- 2026 evidence: HVP components +19.4% organic in Q2; raised guidance. [Actual]\n\nScenario B \u2014 Base: \u201cQuality Compounder Normalizes\u201d (50%)\n- 2026\u20132027: 9\u201310% growth; then 6\u20135% CAGR; EBIT margin plateaus ~21%.\n- EPS FY2029 \u2248 $9.50\u2013$10.50; exit 30x \u2192 $285\u2013$315; discounted \u2192 **$225\u2013$250**.\n- Evidence: consensus forward growth +6.3% [Estimate]; management guides only FY26, not beyond. [Unconfirmed for later years]\n\nScenario C \u2014 Bear: \u201cGrowth Compresses / De-rating\u201d (25%)\n- GLP-1 decelerates, competition arrives, tariffs/reshoring raise costs, cyber incident lingers; revenue growth 3\u20134% post-2026; margins flat/slightly down.\n- EPS FY2029 \u2248 $7.00\u2013$8.00; exit 22x \u2192 $155\u2013$175; discounted \u2192 **$120\u2013$140**.\n- Evidence: 2024\u201325 de-rating episode (55x\u219230x) is precedent; elasto-mer/tariff pressures noted. [Estimate]\n\n4-3. Bayesian update: Prior success probability of double-digit growth out to 2029 was 40%; Q2 2026 data raised it to 55%, and guidance raise to 60%; the counterweight of competitor capacity and tariffs caps it at 60%. [Assumption]\n\n4-4. Expected price (probability-weighted):\nEV = 0.25 \u00d7 $400 + 0.50 \u00d7 $238 + 0.25 \u00d7 $130 = $100 + $119 + $32.5 = **$251.50** [Arithmetic]\nUsing conservative ends of ranges: 0.25\u00d7$385 + 0.50\u00d7$225 + 0.25\u00d7$120 = 96 + 113 + 30 = $239.\nPoint IV = **$245**; we round to $240 for the decision framework. 50% CI: $180\u2013$310; 80% CI: $130\u2013$400.\n\n4-5. MoS = (240 \u2212 353.08)/240 = \u221247% \u2192 Margin of Safety: **None**.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nMarket-implied horizon: At 45x trailing earnings and 57x trailing FCF, the price embeds >5 years of flawless compounding. Our evidence horizon is 1\u20132 years (FY26 guidance) and a presumption of 5\u20137% growth thereafter. Spread = \u22123 years. No arbitrage exists at T0; entry only below $260.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\n5-1/5-2. Conviction Score (0\u20133 each; total /15):\nBusiness quality: 3 | Valuation attractiveness: 0 | Macro alignment: 2 | Thesis clarity: 2 | Execution traceability: 1 (cyber event) \u2192 **8/15 (Medium)** \u2014 with \u22120.5 step from macro-lag/execution, effective 7.5/15 (Medium-Low). Conviction decay: re-check every 6 months; \u22121 point if no guidance extension beyond FY26. [Framework rule]\n\n5-3. Sizing: Medium-Low conviction \u2192 max weight 3%, but negative MoS \u2192 no new position. Existing holders: keep \u22642%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n- Short interest: 3.92% of float, 2.9 days to cover, slight MoM build +3.2% [Actual \u2013 pack/stale 15 days]\n- Institutional ownership: 1.04% [Actual \u2013 pack; likely 13F subset, treat as unreliable] [Unconfirmed]\n- Insider ownership: 0.64% [Actual \u2013 pack]\n- Insiders/company buyback: $454.3M H1 2026 at avg $258.03 \u2014 strong signal below $300. [Actual]\n- Analyst targets: mean $404.73 / median $410 / high $459 / low $340 [Actual \u2013 stale 15 days]\n- Options/sentiment: not available [Unconfirmed]\n- Crowding score: 11/15 \u2014 GLP-1 \u201cpicks-and-shovels\u201d theme is crowded; stock near 52-week high ($386 vs $353 = 8.5% below high). \u2192 Sizing deduction: 1pp.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\nWST adds: quality, healthcare defense, weak-dollar tailwind, low correlation to cyclical value, zero balance-sheet risk (net cash). Deducts: no dividend bargain (0.25% yield), valuation risk, crowding. If portfolio already holds GLP-1 exposure (LLY, NVO, or supplier names), WST adds correlated concentration. Fit: Moderate \u2014 acceptable only as a small quality sleeve in a diversified book; size cap 2%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nInputs: expected excess return = (IV \u2212 P)/P = (240 \u2212 353)/353 = \u221232%; variance of 1-year outcomes \u2248 (25%)^2 = 6.25%. Kelly fraction = edge / odds = negative \u2192 **0%** [Arithmetic]. Kelly is inconclusive when edge is negative; the only correct size is zero for new money. This differs from Layer 5 cap (2%) by >20pp \u2192 re-validation: Layer 5 cap applies only to existing positions as a trim ceiling, not new buying. Framework rule satisfied.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\nCatalysts\n- FY2026 H2 continue above guidance; Q3 guide at $820\u2013$835M / EPS $2.14\u2013$2.24 [Actual]\n- HVP Components maintaining >15% organic growth through 2026 [Actual \u2013 Q2]\n- 10-Year yield falling below 4% / Fed easing \u2192 multiple expansion [Assumption]\n- GLP-1 label expansions (oral, new indications, pediatric) expand packaging demand [Assumption]\n\nRisks\n- Cybersecurity incident (May 4\u20137, 2026, 8-K [Actual]): systems encrypted, data exfiltrated, operations partially restarted. Remediation/legal costs not quantified; could hit FY27 capex/opex. [Estimate]\n- WestVantage revenue trough Q3 2026 (contract exit), recovery only in Q4 [Actual \u2013 PR]\n- US pharma tariffs/onshoring \u2192 capex location costs, margin drag [Estimate]\n- Elastomer capacity additions (Datwyler, Gerresheimer, SGD) [Estimate]\n- GLP-1 oral formulations or pricing pressure reduce device attach rates [Assumption]\n\nPre-mortem (7-4): If WST is a loser five years from now, the likely story is: (1) GLP-1 growth normalized and the multiple de-rated to 22x; (2) cyber incident costs exceeded $200M and triggered customer de-qualifications; (3) mainline standard products deflated on competition. Early-warning indicators: HVP organic growth < 10% for two consecutive quarters; gross margin < 34%; free-cash-flow margin < 10%; announcement of major customer internalizing production.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\nCommon bear triggers across portfolio: dollar strength reversal (tariff war), GLP-1 class safety event, healthcare sector de-rating. Tail correlation: healthcare quality names historically show >0.7 correlation in drawdowns (2020, 2022). CVaR (95%) for a 25% position \u2248 \u221230% portfolio impact at cap weight. Recommendation: cap WST exposure at 2% to keep CVaR contribution \u2264 \u22120.6%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES (Thesis Integrity KPIs)\n\n- HVP Components organic growth: must remain \u226510% each quarter. Break = re-evaluate thesis.\n- Gross margin: must remain \u226535% (TTM). Break = reassess pricing.\n- FCF conversion: Adjusted FCF (OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC) \u2265 11% of revenue. Break = reassess working-capital model.\n- Capex discipline: keep $250\u2013$350M/yr; >$400M without revenue acceleration = red flag.\n- Cyber incident: track remediation/insurance; any customer loss >1% of revenue = re-evaluate.\n- Guidance cadence: continue raising or maintaining FY guidance; withdrawal = red flag.\n- 10-Yr UST: if <3.8%, review DCF upward; if >5.3%, review valuation multiple cap.\n- Competitor capacity announcements: track Datwyler/Gerresheimer/SGD elastomer expansions [Unconfirmed \u2013 not finalized in this research].\n- Monitoring cadence: quarterly earnings; semi-annual conviction decay review; annual full re-run.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM / ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\n9-1. Short thesis (7 bullets)\n1. Market cap $24.9B on TTM FCF ~$413M \u21d2 60x FCF. Paying 60x cash flow for an 8% fundamental grower is mathematically imprudent even with a 15% FCF margin path.\n2. GLP-1 is the most crowded trade in healthcare; a single regulatory/pricing shock would trigger a 30\u201340% de-rating (see 2024\u201325 WST precedent from 55x to 30x).\n3. The 70% elastomer share invites capacity competition; both Datwyler and Gerresheimer are adding capacity and could price under WST for ready-to-use formats.\n4. Cyber incident: systems encrypted, data exfiltrated \u2014 remediation, legal, regulatory costs unknown and likely multi-year.\n5. Tariff-driven reshoring of pharma manufacturing will force WST to add U.S. capacity at high cost; capex guidance will rise faster than sales.\n6. The FY26 guidance (+10\u201311% organic) \u2014 even if beat \u2014 still values the backlog; no post-27 guide exists; consensus +6.3% may be too high if GLP-1 doses are spaced (e.g., monthly) or oral drugs progress.\n7. Balance-sheet \u201cquality\u201d is pricing in zero risk; the company is not immune to a working-capital reversal: WC jumped from $988M (2024) to $1,323M (2025), a $335M drag \u2014 repeated, this destroys DCF math.\n\n9-2. Logic combat: Long logical strength 12/20; Short logical strength 13/20. Gap = 1 < 2 \u2192 conviction downgraded one step (from Medium to Medium-Low). [Framework rule]\n\n9-3. Adversarial data: Hindenburg or activist campaigns: none found [Unconfirmed]. The SEC 8-K cyber disclosure is the only adversarial-compliance event.\n\n9-4. Killed arguments\n- \u201cMargins will reach 25%\u201d: plausible only if HVP exceeds 60% of mix and no price war occurs. If competitor capacity comes online, margin upside is capped at 21\u201322%.\n- \u201cGLP-1 will grow 20% forever\u201d: base rates for drug niches show 5\u20137 year surges, then normalization.\n- \u201cNet cash makes it defensive\u201d: true, but cash does not protect the multiple in a growth-stock de-rating.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\nA. Methodology audit: Engine 1 appropriate; no option/hybrid premium used [Pass]\nB. Valuation audit